    "summary": "Emotional He's Gone > Spoonful jam and soaring second‑set jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a moving first‑time performance of He’s Gone after Brent’s passing, followed by an expansive China Cat → Rider into a cathartic He’s Gone → Spoonful sequence. Jerry’s guitar and vocals are on fire, Phil’s slapping bass drives the jams, while Vince provides tasteful keys. The audience recording is exceptionally clear, making it the preferred source.",
    "review": "September 11, 1990 at the Spectrum captured one of the most heartfelt nights of the early‑90s. After a solid first set that opened with Jack Straw and rode through Bertha and Hell In A Bucket, the band launched into an emotionally charged second set. The China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue segued into Looks Like Rain before launching into He’s Gone → Spoonful, a sequence reviewers describe as “raw” and “cathartic,” with Jerry’s guitar tone and vocal delivery on fire and Phil’s funky, slapping bass anchoring the improvisation. The momentum kept rolling through The Wheel, I Need A Miracle, Black Peter and an exuberant Around & Around, while Vince’s keyboards managed to honor Brent’s legacy despite mixed opinions about his fit. The audience tape (gd90-09-11.fob‑nak300.wiley.11808.sbeok.shnf) stands out for its crisp, clean mix that captures the Wall of Sound without distortion, delivering an immersive listening experience that lets the band’s grief and joy shine through.\n\nOverall, the performance is a poignant snapshot of the Dead navigating loss while still launching into the soaring jams fans crave. Jerry’s emotive playing, Phil’s driving bass, Mickey’s “evil best” drumming paired with Bill’s tight back‑beat, and Vince’s solid keys combine to make this show a must‑listen for anyone interested in that transitional period of the band.",
